Pause sensor polling across system sleep

A sensor keeps polling while the system sleeps as long as a client
holds its claim, and clients do not reliably release their claims
before a sleep. On the Surface Pro 11 (Snapdragon X1E), whose sensors
sit behind the ADSP's Sensor Core, an open sensor stream makes the
ADSP signal the CPUs about once a second, and the first signal ends a
suspend to RAM: the tablet woke 2-4 s after every sleep that began
with a stream open, and with the cover closed it went back to sleep
and woke again in a loop.

Stop every sensor when logind sends PrepareForSleep(true) and start
the claimed ones again on PrepareForSleep(false). A "delay" inhibitor
lock, held while the system is awake and released once the sensors
are stopped, makes logind wait for that before the system sleeps.
Without logind nothing changes. A first claim made during the sleep
is answered with the first reading after the wake, as first claims
are otherwise answered with the first reading.

Polling is now decided in one place, reconcile_polling(), from each
sensor's clients and whether the system is going to sleep; the claim,
release and hotplug paths call it instead of driver_set_polling(). A
driver may iterate the main context while it starts or stops a sensor
(the libssc drivers do, through libssc's synchronous calls), and a
claim, release or sleep notification handled meanwhile now only makes
the running reconcile_polling() go round again, so no driver is
started or stopped re-entrantly. Sensors claimed while the proxy was
still opening them start once they are open. On exit the drivers are
closed as before, with no polling decision taken meanwhile.

The integration test runs logind's mock and checks the lock, the
pause, the resume, a release during the sleep and a claim made during
the sleep.
